An event emitted from inside a lambda reaches the caller's handler.

passInlinePure substitutes a component's `emit` with the body the call site
wrote for that event. The walk that finds those emits knew every body a node
carries -- children, prop lambdas, handlers, slots -- and then treated an
assignment, a call statement, a local declaration and a return as leaves. A
lambda written in one of their expressions is a body too, and it was the one
body the walk never entered, so `run(func() { picked() })` reached codegen
still spelled as a call to `emit`, a function no target defines.

The shape is not about timers, though that is where it was found: html's timer
override arms setInterval with a lambda that emits `tick`. Any component that
hands a callback to a function has it.

One unrelated defect is visible in the goldens below and deliberately left
alone, since it is not this walk: html leaves a listener for the component
event on the element even though the substitution has removed the only emit of
it. android typed a func-valued parameter as `Any` and then called it, which
did not compile, until Kotlin spelled a function type as one.

deny * `emit\(` -- an emit is substituted away by lowering; one reaching a backend is a call to a function no target defines
-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
    }
    none {
        html
    }
    kotlin {
        android
    }
}

func run(f func()) {
    f()
}

component chooser(@picked) node {
    button(text="pick", @click {
        run(func() {
            picked()
        })
    })
}

component main node {
    var picks = 0

    chooser(@picked { picks += 1 })
    text(value="picks=" + string(picks))
}

window {
    main
}
